Basic concept:
	POSIXy unless stated otherwise
	Microkernel servers, special files, other objects can be queried for what interfaces they support
	An interface is a UUID representing the OOP-style interface supported by an object or server
	Interfaces are used both within the same address space (where a tool can autogen code for it) and between spaces/processes
	System is heavily split into different domains, similar to a hypervisor, and processes get different views

Object stores can be saved to any block device, and a "filesystem view" of an object store can be created using an adapter
For example, a filesystem view could represent all IImage objects in a particular object store

No global registry of objects, but object stores can be mounted and appear in the VFS as:

	/objects/StoreUUID/ObjectUUID
	
	This can be exported into the POSIX view or a pure POSIX domain so that traditional *nix apps can work with the object model

Adapters can allow different views, so for example a filesystem could be adapted into an object store using something like the standard UNIX file(1) to figure out file types and representing files as objects
Files that can't be identified would just be a generic IByteStream or whatever

Interfaces like ITextFile or ITextStream can exist too


A simple userspace lib allows querying an object for an interface and returns the relevant C struct or a generic struct for invoking methods etc
If the object is within the same space, it goes direct, otherwise IPC magic happens

Inspirations:
	COM & UEFI

domains:
	domains are given access to only part of the hardware, including a slice of RAM or the ability to allocate from parent domain
	domains can be "pure FacetOS" or "pure POSIX" and processes can be given a "POSIX view"
	a "pure POSIX" domain is just a POSIX view with its own /etc and everything and where PID 1 is a POSIX process
	POSIX domains and other *nix systems running inside VMs can import from a FacetOS domain using NFS and NIS

POSIX view:
	a process can be started with a "POSIX view" where it can see the same process table etc, but it also sees a read-only /etc that is dynamically generated from the FacetOS configuration

virtual consoles:
	idea is something like this can be possible:
		alt-F1 is Domain 0, pure FacetOS login and shell
		alt-F2 is a POSIX view of Domain 0
		alt-F3 is a pure POSIX domain with its own /sbin/init as PID1 running getty for logins etc
		alt-F4 is a VM for something like an OpenBSD VM that exists to provide networking support etc
		alt-F5 is a VM with a tiny Linux presenting wayland/X11 for graphics etc

dominit0 and child domains:
	dominit0 is configured using a multiboot2 module config file and initrd & command-line arguments
	a child domain is basically spawned as a subset of what the root task gets, with a few caps added for stuff like domain management, memory allocation, klog and so on
	the caps are passed in by telling the child dominit0 it's a child with a command-line argument giving it the domain ID
	by default, if not otherwise specified, dominit0 assumes it's domain ID 0, so in theory an entire FacetOS tree of domains can be virtualized

	domains are told that they have domain management authority, either of their own children, or of siblings (by delegating up to their parent)
	domains can also be told they don't have domain management authority - though technically nothing stops a child domain from doing so anyway, since domains are userland, dominit0 is "well behaved"

